<h1>First experience on Open BSD</h1>

<p> After some painful experiences on debian I finally tried running an open bsd server.  The main pain point of debian was the packages.  The disadvantage of having frozen packages is you inevitably run into compatibility issues with software.  The normal solution is to download the binary or PPE which defeats the whole pretence of debain stability.  If packages are frozen for the sake of stability - wouldn't downloading newer versions of the package introduce instability?  Even if it's marginal - downloading an OS and not being able to use the standard package manager is such an absurdity that I decided to pass</p>

<p>Open BSD instantly appealed to me, just that base simplicity - it's a server, it's trying to be a server, and nothing else. Everything seemed to just work.  One issue I ran into was that OpenBSD servers were designed to be run from a nologon user for sercurity.  Except when you start the server as a service from the nologon user it has problems finding the static files and dependencies.  I tried changing the routes in my go executable to no avail.  Finally I used the embed package as a quick and dirty solution.  I think I'll need a better fix for this eventually as larger images, videos and most importantly for private keys.  I think OpenBSD actually lets you set a user's home directory but I'll have to look into it as time allows. One step at a time.<p> 
